WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.830
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:10.389
Imagine that you are a film critic and you
are recording a video review of a movie, but

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:16.120
unfortunately you are not the best kind of
movie critic, and you record it before watching

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.120
the movie.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.550
But here is the problem - you don’t really
know if it’s going to be any good.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:28.260
So you record this.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.690
So far so good.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.220
Nothing too crazy going on here.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:37.260
However, you go in, watch the movie, and it
turns out to be amazing.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.969
So, what do we do if we don’t have time
to re-record the video?

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:50.089
Well, we grab this AI, type in the new text,
and it will give us this.

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:53.420
Whoa!

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.059
What just happened?

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:57.239
What kind of black magic is this?

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:02.440
Well, let’s look behind the person, on the
blackboard you see some delicious partial

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:08.120
derivatives, and I am starting to think that
this person is not a movie critic.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:14.340
And of course, of course he isn’t, because
this is Yoshua Bengio, legendary machine learning

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.750
researcher.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.470
And this was an introduction video where he
says this.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:26.000
And, what happened is that it has been repurposed
by this new DeepFake generator AI, where we

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.700
can type in anything we wish, and out comes
a near-perfect result.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.030
It synthesizes both the video and audio content
for us.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.440
But we are not quite done yet.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.840
Something is missing.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.480
If the movie gets an A+, the gestures of the
subject also have reflect that this is a favorable

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.480
review.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.610
So, what do we do?

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.610
Maybe add a smile there.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.190
Is that possible?

00:01:55.190 --> 00:02:01.680
Oh yes, there we go!

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.680
Amazing.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:08.560
Let’s have a closer look at one more example
where we see how easily we can drop in new

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:16.580
text with this editor.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:24.140
Now, this is not the first method performing
this task - previous techniques typically

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:28.620
required hours and hours of video from a target
subject.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.680
So how much training data does this require
to perform all this?

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.620
Well, let’s have a look together!

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:42.239
Look, this is not the same footage copy-pasted
three times, this is a synthesized video output

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:47.730
if we have 10 minutes of video data from the
test subject.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:55.989
This looks nearly as good, has fewer sharp
details, but, in return, this only requires

00:02:55.989 --> 00:02:57.730
2.5 minutes.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.989
And here comes the best part.

00:02:59.989 --> 00:03:03.290
If you look here, you may be able to see the
difference.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:09.079
And if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper because synthesizing

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:14.849
this only required 30 seconds of video footage
of the target subject.

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:15.969
My goodness.

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:19.459
But we are not nearly done yet, it can do
more!

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:26.750
For instance, it can tone up or down the intensity
of gestures to match the tone of what is being

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.010
said.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.280
Look.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.590
So how does this wizardry happen?

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:37.669
Well, this new technique improves two things
really well, one is that it can search for

00:03:37.669 --> 00:03:40.120
phonemes and other units better.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:45.689
Here is an example, we crossed out the word
“spider” and wish to use the word “fox”

00:03:45.689 --> 00:03:51.870
instead, and it tries to assemble this word
from previous occurrences of individual sounds.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:58.469
For instance, the “ox” part is available
when the test subject utters the word “box”.

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:03.349
And two, it can then stitch them together
better than previous methods.

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:08.640
And surely this means that since it needs
less data, the synthesis must take a great

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.790
deal longer, right?

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:16.639
No, not at all, the synthesis part only takes
40 seconds.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:21.220
And even if it couldn’t this so quickly,
the performance control aspect where we can

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:27.700
tone the gestures up or down, or add a smile
would still be an amazing selling point in

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:28.879
and of itself.

00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:36.080
But no, it does all of these things quickly,
and with high quality at the same time.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.080
Wow.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:42.530
I now invite you to look at the results carefully,
and give them a hard time.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.060
Did you find anything out of ordinary?

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.979
Did you find this believable?

00:04:46.979 --> 00:04:48.840
Let me know in the comments below.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.319
The authors of the paper also conducted a
user study with a 110 participants, who were

00:04:54.319 --> 00:05:00.120
asked to look at 25 videos and say which one
they felt was real.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:06.300
The results showed that the new method outperforms
previous techniques, even if they have access

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.050
to 12 times more training data.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:13.639
Which is absolutely absolutely amazing, but
what is even better, the longer the video

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:16.860
clips were, the better this method fared.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.360
What a time to be alive!

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:25.289
Now, of course, beyond the many amazing use-cases
of DeepFakes in reviving deceased actors,

00:05:25.289 --> 00:05:30.409
creating beautiful visual art, redubbing movies,
and more, we have to be vigilant about the

00:05:30.409 --> 00:05:33.349
fact that they can also be used for nefarious
purposes.

00:05:33.349 --> 00:05:38.639
The goal of this video is to let you, and
the public know that these DeepFakes can now

00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:44.300
be created quickly and inexpensively, and
they don’t require a trained scientist anymore.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:49.759
If this can be done, it is of utmost importance
that we all know about it!

00:05:49.759 --> 00:05:54.919
And beyond that, whenever they invite me,
I inform key political and military decision

00:05:54.919 --> 00:06:00.110
makers about the existence and details of
these techniques to make sure that they also

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:05.610
know about these and using that knowledge,
they can make better decisions for us.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.680
You can see me doing that here.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.570
Note that these talks and consultations all
happen free of charge, and if they keep inviting

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:30.449
me, I’ll keep showing up to help with this
in the future as a service 

00:06:30.449 --> 00:07:02.689
to the public.

00:07:02.689 --> 00:07:10.621
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

